        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: In strict products liability, an intervening omission by an intermediary, assembler, or employer—such as a failure to inspect, test, or discover a latent defect—is an ordinary and foreseeable intervening force that does NOT constitute a superseding cause. A commercial component manufacturer who places an unreasonably dangerous, defectively manufactured material into the stream of commerce remains strictly liable for injuries caused by the defect when incorporated into an end product without substantial alteration. The tent maker's failure to perform standard trade temperature checks is foreseeable and does not sever the fabric manufacturer's proximate liability (Option B). Option A is incorrect because a failure to inspect is not a superseding cause. Option C is incorrect because the sophisticated user defense applies to warnings for industrial chemicals, not manufacturing defects in physical components. Option D is incorrect because privity of contract is not required in tort strict products liability."
