        exp: "Rule: Under the common law of contracts (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 84 and § 150), a party to a written contract within the Statute of Frauds may orally waive an express condition or time for performance. While private no-oral-modification clauses are recognized, common law courts hold that parties may orally waive a timing condition, and that an executory oral waiver becomes completely IRREVOCABLE if the other party has materially and detrimentally changed his position in reliance on the waiver prior to receiving notice of retraction. The developer foreseeably and detrimentally changed position by letting its rate lock expire in reliance on the oral agreement. The investor is equitably estopped from enforcing the December 1 deadline, entitling the developer to specific performance (Option B). Options A and C are incorrect because detrimental reliance makes an oral waiver binding and irrevocable without a writing or new consideration. Option D is incorrect because time-of-the-essence clauses are valid in real estate contracts."
