        fp: "A manufacturer of high-end consumer fireworks designed an aerial display shell equipped with an internal chemical pyrotechnic fuse. The manufacturer complied with all federal consumer product safety standards, and package warnings prominently stated: 'DANGER: Stand back 100 feet. Light fuse and retire immediately.' A consumer purchased the aerial shell from a licensed retailer for a Fourth of July backyard celebration. The consumer placed the shell into a launch tube in his backyard. The consumer lit the fuse with a match. Due to a manufacturing defect in the chemical composition of the fuse, the fuse burned instantaneously at 1,000 feet per second rather than the standard 5 seconds. The shell detonated while the consumer was still standing directly over the launch tube, blowing the launch tube apart and severing the consumer's right hand. A neighbor sitting on a lawn chair on his own patio 120 feet away was struck in the chest by a jagged metal fragment from the exploding tube, suffering a punctured lung. Both the consumer and the neighbor brought a strict products liability action against the fireworks manufacturer.",
